---
import PageLayout from '../../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import LegalIdentity from '../../components/LegalIdentity.astro';
import { route } from '../../i18n/routes';
import { AUTHOR_NAME, AUTHOR_DESC, CONTACT_EMAIL, SITE_NAMES } from '../../data/site-config';
import { srcLink } from '../../lib/helpers';
import { KIT as K } from '../../lib/kit';
import { formatNumber } from '../../lib/format';
import { profilePageSchema, breadcrumbSchema } from '../../lib/seo';
const lang = 'en';
const path = route('about', lang);
const title = 'DE Verkaufsoffen 2026: publisher, sources, independence';
const description = 'Who publishes DE Verkaufsoffen 2026: Radif Partners, independent and promoting no shop. Where the state acts, the town ordinances and all the dates come from.';
const s = (k: string, t: string) => srcLink(k, lang, t);
---
<PageLayout title={title} description={description} lang={lang} jsonLd={[profilePageSchema({ path, lang }), breadcrumbSchema([{ name: 'Home', path: route('home', lang) }, { name: 'About', path }])]} breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'About' }]}>
  <article class="prose prose-slate max-w-none">
    <h1>About {SITE_NAMES[lang]}</h1>
    <p>{AUTHOR_DESC.en}</p>
    <h2>Why this site exists</h2>
    <p>Anyone wanting to know whether shops in their town open on Sunday finds plenty of lists, but rarely the town’s own decision. Some lists carry dates that were cancelled long ago, others roll last year’s dates forward. This site does the reverse: it lists only what a town has officially published, links the source and says openly where nothing has been published yet. It also explains, for each of the sixteen states, what the law allows.</p>
    <h2>Who publishes it</h2>
    <p>The publisher is {AUTHOR_NAME}, a company that builds tools from public rules and data. {AUTHOR_NAME} is not a public authority, a law firm or a retail association, has no link with any town or state government and uses no coats of arms or logos. The site promotes no shop, chain or shopping centre and contains no affiliate links.</p>
    <LegalIdentity lang={lang} />
    <h2>Where the data comes from</h2>
    <p>The rules come from the sixteen shop opening acts, such as the <Fragment set:html={s('lo-nw', 'North Rhine-Westphalia act')} /> or the <Fragment set:html={s('lo-sn', 'Saxon act')} />; the constitutional frame from <Fragment set:html={s('gg-140', 'Art. 140 of the Basic Law')} /> and the <Fragment set:html={s('bverfg-2009', 'Federal Constitutional Court judgment of 2009')} />. The dates come from the ordinances, general orders and official gazettes of {formatNumber(K.STAEDTE.length, 0, lang)} towns. How we check is under <a href={route('method', lang)}>method</a>; how we correct, in the <a href={route('editorial', lang)}>editorial policy</a>.</p>
    <h2>Your data</h2>
    <p>The finder and all calculators run in your browser. The town, state and period you choose are neither sent nor stored; the calendar file is created on your device. There is no account and no form. Details are in the <a href={route('privacy', lang)}>privacy policy</a>.</p>
    <h2>What the site does not do</h2>
    <p>It does not replace the town’s notice or legal advice. Whether an individual shop actually opens on a released Sunday is up to that shop. We add short-notice cancellations and court rulings as soon as the town publishes them.</p>
    <h2>Write to us</h2>
    <p><a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a> if a date is missing, cancelled or wrong.</p>
  </article>
</PageLayout>
